  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Plenty of cool air aloft has poured southward into \nthe region, with morning strato-cu continuing to slowly mix upwards. \n High temperatures given the cloud cover will remain in the upper \n70s to lower 80s. Aloft, the increasing northwesterly flow could \nbring high level smoke though the region into the night. \n\nTONIGHT: Surface high pressure drifts into the Midwest and western \nGreat Lakes region overnight.  Winds turn light and variable which, \nmay lead to fairly cool overnight lows in areas east of the James \nRiver.  The low-lvl airmass is fairly dry, so fog probabilities will \nremain very localized to sheltered valleys and riverways. \nFurther west, high level clouds increase after midnight as mid-\nlvl warm advection focuses over the West River areas and the \nNebraska panhandle. A few sprinkles to light rain showers may \nreach the central portions of SD into the James River valley by\ndaybreak.\n\nWEDNESDAY:  A subtle shortwave drops southeast from the Black Hills \nregion across central Nebraska during Wednesday. Much of the \nCWA will remain under the influence of low-lvl dry air advection\nfrom the northeast during the day. While mid-lvl lift spread \neast towards the I-29 corridor, most guidance struggles to \nproduce more than sprinkles east of I-29, with the best low-lvl \nsaturation focused along and south of the MO River valley. Any \nQPF that falls will be light. The most impact from this system \nmay be increased cloud cover and temperatures in the 70s and \n80s. \n\nTHURSDAY: After another night with variable cloud cover, light \nwinds, and cooler temperatures, Thursday will see surface winds \nreturn back to the South early in the day. We'll be watching another \nslightly stronger wave cross the Rockies during the day on Thursday. \nModel guidance has been a bit all over with this wave, with the GFS \nand about 40% of it's ensemble members stubbornly further to the NE \nthan all of the other major model guidance. The ECMWF/ICON/GEM/UKMET \nand to some degree NAM force this wave further southeast bringing \nthe majority of rain chances to western SD, central and \nnorthern NE and western Iowa. The latest NBM has hedged a bit \nmore towards the drier solutions as more drier input is \nreceived, and I continue to favor the greatest focus for rain \nalong the MO River valley. That said, if there is some shift \ntowards the outlier solutions, moisture convergence could be \nenough to at least produce some meaningful rain into Thursday \nnight. \n\nFRIDAY-MONDAY: A 600 DM ridge builds through the Central Plains \nheading into the upcoming weekend.  This ridge, unlike the past \nweeks, it a bit more suppressed which may serve to delay the most \nintense heat into the second half of the weekend.  Temperatures are \nlikely to surge into the 90s Saturday and Sunday with initial focus \nfor the hottest temperatures west of the James River Saturday and \nthen further east Sunday.  Even with the slightly warm biased NBM, \nthere is some potential for heat related headlines this weekend. \nConvection chances remain low for Saturday, but may rise slightly on \nthe edge of the EML late Sunday. A very hot Monday is also \npossible with a similar setup for weak flow convection on the \nedge of the EML in the afternoon evening.",
